From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 11:51:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-gpu-virgl: honour INFO_RING_IDX with context fences

A Venus guest that negotiated CONTEXT_INIT sends its fenced SUBMIT_3D
with VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_FENCE | VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_INFO_RING_IDX: a fence on
one ring of that context. virgl_process_cmd() ignored ring_idx and
always created a ctx0 fence with virgl_renderer_create_fence(). On the
RPi4 (AAOS WebView, ~150 such fences a second) that meant an EGL sync
created on the main thread per fence, polled every millisecond, and a
fence that signals when vrend's GL work is done instead of when the
Venus context reaches it.

Create a per-context fence (virgl_renderer_context_create_fence,
mergeable) for those commands and retire them from the v3
write_context_fence callback, every pending fence of that ctx/ring up to
the reported id. ctx0 retirement skips them. If virglrenderer refuses
the context fence the command falls back to a ctx0 fence as before. The
callback struct is raised to version 3 (still 4 with an EGL display).

virglrenderer frees a context's pending context fences when the context
is destroyed, without reporting them, and a guest process that exits or
is killed sends CTX_DESTROY without waiting for its fences. So
CTX_DESTROY, once the context is gone, answers every command still
waiting on a fence of one of its rings. Left in the queue they would
never be answered (leaking the guest's descriptors and keeping the fence
poll timer running), and a new context reusing the ctx_id would retire
them.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
+++ b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
@@ -550,6 +550,35 @@
     virgl_renderer_context_create(cc.hdr.ctx_id, cc.nlen, cc.debug_name);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Answer the commands waiting on a fence of one of ctx_id's rings
+ * (INFO_RING_IDX): those of ring ring_idx up to fence_id, or with any_ring
+ * all of them.
+ */
+static void virgl_retire_ring_fences(VirtIOGPU *g, uint32_t ctx_id,
+                                     bool any_ring, uint32_t ring_idx,
+                                     uint64_t fence_id)
+{
+    struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *cmd, *tmp;
+
+    QTAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE(cmd, &g->fenceq, next, tmp) {
+        if (!(cmd->cmd_hdr.flags & VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_INFO_RING_IDX) ||
+            cmd->cmd_hdr.ctx_id != ctx_id ||
+            (!any_ring && cmd->cmd_hdr.ring_idx != ring_idx) ||
+            cmd->cmd_hdr.fence_id > fence_id) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        trace_virtio_gpu_fence_resp(cmd->cmd_hdr.fence_id);
+        virtio_gpu_ctrl_response_nodata(g, cmd, VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_OK_NODATA);
+        QTAILQ_REMOVE(&g->fenceq, cmd, next);
+        g_free(cmd);
+        g->inflight--;
+        if (virtio_gpu_stats_enabled(g->parent_obj.conf)) {
+            trace_virtio_gpu_dec_inflight_fences(g->inflight);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
 static void virgl_cmd_context_destroy(VirtIOGPU *g,
                                       struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *cmd)
 {
@@ -559,6 +588,12 @@
     trace_virtio_gpu_cmd_ctx_destroy(cd.hdr.ctx_id);
 
     virgl_renderer_context_destroy(cd.hdr.ctx_id);
+    /*
+     * virglrenderer frees the context's pending ring fences without reporting
+     * them, so answer those commands now: otherwise they stay in fenceq
+     * forever (and a new context reusing ctx_id would retire them instead).
+     */
+    virgl_retire_ring_fences(g, cd.hdr.ctx_id, true, 0, UINT64_MAX);
 }
 
 static void virtio_gpu_rect_update(VirtIOGPU *g, int idx, int x, int y,
@@ -1177,6 +1212,20 @@
     }
 
     trace_virtio_gpu_fence_ctrl(cmd->cmd_hdr.fence_id, cmd->cmd_hdr.type);
+    /*
+     * A fence on one of the context's rings (Venus timelines) belongs to that
+     * context: it signals when the context reaches it, which a ctx0 (vrend)
+     * fence does not express. It also spares the main thread an EGL sync per
+     * fence. Fall back to ctx0 only if virglrenderer refuses it.
+     */
+    if ((cmd->cmd_hdr.flags & VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_INFO_RING_IDX) &&
+        !virgl_renderer_context_create_fence(cmd->cmd_hdr.ctx_id,
+                                             VIRGL_RENDERER_FENCE_FLAG_MERGEABLE,
+                                             cmd->cmd_hdr.ring_idx,
+                                             cmd->cmd_hdr.fence_id)) {
+        return;
+    }
+    cmd->cmd_hdr.flags &= ~VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_INFO_RING_IDX;
     virgl_renderer_create_fence(cmd->cmd_hdr.fence_id, cmd->cmd_hdr.type);
 }
 
@@ -1193,6 +1242,10 @@
         if (cmd->cmd_hdr.fence_id > fence) {
             continue;
         }
+        /* context-ring fences are retired by virgl_write_context_fence() */
+        if (cmd->cmd_hdr.flags & VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_INFO_RING_IDX) {
+            continue;
+        }
         trace_virtio_gpu_fence_resp(cmd->cmd_hdr.fence_id);
         virtio_gpu_ctrl_response_nodata(g, cmd, VIRTIO_GPU_RESP_OK_NODATA);
         QTAILQ_REMOVE(&g->fenceq, cmd, next);
@@ -1237,9 +1290,22 @@
                                    qctx);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Per-context fence (v3 callback). Fences signal in order within one ring, and with
+ * VIRGL_RENDERER_FENCE_FLAG_MERGEABLE virglrenderer may report only the latest, so
+ * retire every pending fence of that ring up to fence_id.
+ */
+static void virgl_write_context_fence(void *opaque, uint32_t ctx_id,
+                                      uint32_t ring_idx, uint64_t fence_id)
+{
+    virgl_retire_ring_fences(opaque, ctx_id, false, ring_idx, fence_id);
+}
+
 static struct virgl_renderer_callbacks virtio_gpu_3d_cbs = {
-    .version             = 1,
+    /* >= 3 for write_context_fence; raised to 4 below when an EGL display is set */
+    .version             = 3,
     .write_fence         = virgl_write_fence,
+    .write_context_fence = virgl_write_context_fence,
     .create_gl_context   = virgl_create_context,
     .destroy_gl_context  = virgl_destroy_context,
     .make_current        = virgl_make_context_current,
